{"p":"/ui/labels","t":"Text & image labels","d":"Explore how labels display customizable text and images in user interfaces.","h":"<p>import TextFiltering from '../includes/text-filtering/text-filtering.md'</p>\n<p><strong>Labels</strong> are <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObjects</code></a> that let you display customizable text and images <a href=\"#/docs/ui/on-screen-containers\">on‑screen</a> or <a href=\"#/docs/ui/in-experience-containers\">in-game</a>. There are two types of labels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel\"><code>TextLabel</code></a> is a rectangle with text that you can style through customizable properties. This is the primary way to display text in a game.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>An <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ImageLabel\"><code>ImageLabel</code></a> is a rectangle with an image <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/projects/assets\">asset</a> that you can style through customizable properties. Alongside <a href=\"#/docs/parts/textures-decals\">textures and decals</a>, this lets you display images in a game.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Create labels on the screen</h2>\n<p>Labels on a screen are useful for things like displaying images of characters\nwith dialog.</p>\n<p>To add a label to a screen:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>In the <strong>Explorer</strong> window, select <strong>StarterGui</strong> and add a <strong>ScreenGui</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hover over StarterGui and click the &CirclePlus; button. A contextual menu displays.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>From the menu, insert a <strong>ScreenGui</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Select the new <strong>ScreenGui</strong> and add a label.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hover over <strong>ScreenGui</strong> and click the &CirclePlus; button. A contextual menu displays.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>From the menu, insert a <strong>TextLabel</strong> or <strong>ImageLabel</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<h2>Create labels on part faces</h2>\n<p>Labels on a part are useful for things like creating billboards, posters, and\nwallpaper.</p>\n<p>To add a label to the face of a part:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>In the <strong>Explorer</strong> window, insert a <strong>SurfaceGui</strong> onto the part.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hover over the part instance and click the &CirclePlus; button. A contextual menu displays.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>From the menu, insert a <strong>SurfaceGui</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Select the new <strong>SurfaceGui</strong> and add a label.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hover over the <strong>SurfaceGui</strong> and click the &CirclePlus; button. A contextual menu displays.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>From the menu, insert a <strong>TextLabel</strong> or <strong>ImageLabel</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<div class=\"callout c-warning\" markdown=\"block\">\n   If you don't see the button, try [choosing a different face](#/docs/parts/textures-decals) in the &lt;b&gt;Face&lt;/b&gt; property of the &lt;b&gt;SurfaceGui&lt;/b&gt;.\n   </div>\n<h2>Adjust ImageLabel properties</h2>\n<p>All images within an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ImageLabel\"><code>ImageLabel</code></a> must be assets that have been <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/projects/assets/manager#asset-import\">imported</a> to Studio. While the image automatically scales to fit the area of the rectangle, the image looks best when it displays at its native resolution.</p>\n<p>You can customize the visual appearance of an image with the following\nproperties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ImageLabel/ImageLabel.ImageColor3\"><code>ImageColor3</code></a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ImageLabel/ImageLabel.ImageTransparency\"><code>ImageTransparency</code></a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.BackgroundTransparency\"><code>BackgroundTransparency</code></a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, to display only the image and hide the rectangle, set the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.BackgroundTransparency\"><code>BackgroundTransparency</code></a> property to <code>1</code>.</p>","kid":{"simple":"Labels are how you show words and pictures in your game's UI. A `TextLabel` is a box with text in it, and an `ImageLabel` is a box with a picture in it. You can put them on the player's screen or on the side of a part, like a poster. Players can look at labels, but they cannot click them; for that you would use a button.","analogy":"Labels are like the signs and stickers in a store: they tell you things and show pictures, but pressing them does nothing.","keyPoints":["`TextLabel` is the main way to show text in a game, and `ImageLabel` shows a picture.","For on-screen labels: add a `ScreenGui` to `StarterGui`, then add a `TextLabel` or `ImageLabel` inside it.","For labels on a part: add a `SurfaceGui` to the part, then add the label inside the `SurfaceGui`.","If you cannot see a label on a part, try a different `Face` on the `SurfaceGui`.","Images must be imported to Studio as assets before an `ImageLabel` can show them.","Images stretch to fit the box but look best at their original size.","`ImageColor3` tints the image, `ImageTransparency` fades it, and `BackgroundTransparency` set to 1 hides the box behind it."],"terms":{"GuiObject":"Any 2D UI piece, like a label, button, or frame.","asset":"A file uploaded to Roblox, like an image, with its own ID.","native resolution":"The image's real size in pixels. A 256 by 256 image looks sharpest when shown at 256 by 256.","transparency":"How see-through something is. 0 is solid, 1 is invisible, 0.5 is half see-through.","ImageColor3":"A color mixed over the image. White leaves it unchanged; red makes it reddish.","SurfaceGui":"A container that puts UI onto one face of a part."},"example":{"title":"Show a coin counter on screen","place":"LocalScript in StarterPlayerScripts","code":"local Players = game:GetService(\"Players\")\n\nlocal player = Players.LocalPlayer\nlocal playerGui = player:WaitForChild(\"PlayerGui\")\n\n-- A ScreenGui holds on-screen UI\nlocal screenGui = Instance.new(\"ScreenGui\")\nscreenGui.Name = \"CoinGui\"\nscreenGui.Parent = playerGui\n\nlocal label = Instance.new(\"TextLabel\")\nlabel.Size = UDim2.fromOffset(200, 50) -- 200 pixels wide, 50 tall\nlabel.Position = UDim2.fromOffset(20, 20) -- 20 pixels from top-left\nlabel.BackgroundTransparency = 1 -- hide the box, keep the text\nlabel.TextColor3 = Color3.fromRGB(255, 215, 0) -- gold\nlabel.TextSize = 32\nlabel.Text = \"Coins: 0\"\nlabel.Parent = screenGui","explain":["We find this player's `PlayerGui`, where on-screen UI lives.","We create a `ScreenGui` to hold our label.","We create a `TextLabel`, size it, and place it near the top-left corner.","We hide the background box, color the text gold, and set the text.","Later, you can change `label.Text` to show the new coin count."]},"watchOut":["Labels cannot be clicked. If you need clicks, use a `TextButton` or `ImageButton`.","An `ImageLabel` with a blank `Image` shows only a plain box. Make sure you set a real image asset ID.","If the white box behind your text looks ugly, set `BackgroundTransparency` to 1."]}}